+## Creating a JSON Patch
+
+`createPatch(from, to)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, returns a JSON Patch (see above) that turns
+`from` into `to` when applied with `applyPatch`.
+
+```ts
+createPatch({ a: 1, b: [1, 2, 3] }, { a: 1, b: [1, 3] }); // [{ op: "remove", path: "/b/1" }]
+```
+
+The patch holds only what changed. Objects are compared member by member and arrays by their longest
+common run of items: an inserted or removed item is an `add` or a `remove`, a moved item is a `remove`
+followed by an `add`, and the items around them are left alone. An array item that changed in place is
+patched inside, and a value whose type changed is replaced. Neither input is mutated and the patch
+shares no structure with them. It does not throw.
+
 ## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
 
 `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies a JSON Merge Patch to `target`
